.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sentinel
is a lightweight Linux system monitoring and resource governance tool.
It inspects system and process state entirely through direct reads of Linux pseudo-filesystems:
.BR /proc ,
.BR sysfs ,
cgroups v2, and kernel pressure stall information (PSI) files.
It requires no running background daemon for interactive queries, and does not depend on external metric agents.

When configured as a daemon or run in watch mode,
.B sentinel
continually monitors system health, tracks resource exhaustion or anomaly thresholds, and records alerts or performs configured policy actions (such as OOM mitigation or priority adjustments).

.SH SUBCOMMANDS

.TP
.BR health " [" \-\-interval " \fIDURATION\fR] [" \-\-loopback "]"
Display a one-screen summary of current system health including CPU usage, memory and swap utilization, load average, disk I/O, network throughput, and hardware sensors.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " DURATION"
Sampling interval used to compute differential rates (for example, \fB500ms\fR, \fB2s\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-loopback
Include loopback interface traffic in network totals.
.RE

.TP
.BR ps " [" \-\-sort " \fIFIELD\fR] [" \-\-json "]"
List processes running on the system with their PID, user, resource consumption, and status.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " FIELD"
Sort order for the process list. Acceptable values are: \fBcpu\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBname\fR, \fBpressure\fR. Default is \fBcpu\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Format output as structured JSON.
.RE

.TP
.BR top " [" \-\-json "]"
Display the top resource-consuming processes and immediately exit.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Format output as structured JSON.
.RE

.TP
.B tui
Launch the interactive terminal dashboard for real-time monitoring and process control.

.TP
.BR watch " [" \-\-interval " \fIDURATION\fR] [" \-\-duration " \fIDURATION\fR]"
Sample the system at high frequency to observe short-lived spikes or anomalies.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " DURATION"
Interval between samples (e.g. \fB100ms\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " DURATION"
Total time to watch before exiting.
.RE

.TP
.BR alerts " [" \-\-last " \fIDURATION\fR]"
Display recorded alert events from recent history.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-last " DURATION"
Show only alerts within the specified trailing window (e.g. \fB30m\fR, \fB1h\fR).
.RE

.TP
.BI inspect " PID"
Display detailed information about a single process, including threads, memory maps, file descriptors, cgroup membership, and PSI metrics.

.TP
.BI kill " PID" " [" \-\-signal " \fISIGNAL\fR] [" \-\-yes "] [" \-\-dry\-run "]"
Send a termination signal to a target process.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " SIGNAL"
Signal name or number to send (e.g., \fBSIGTERM\fR, \fBSIGKILL\fR, \fB15\fR, \fB9\fR). Default is \fBSIGTERM\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip interactive confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Simulate the signal delivery without modifying the target process.
.RE

.TP
.BI suspend " PID" " [" \-\-yes "] [" \-\-dry\-run "]"
Suspend execution of a process using \fBSIGSTOP\fR.

.TP
.BI resume " PID" " [" \-\-yes "] [" \-\-dry\-run "]"
Resume execution of a suspended process using \fBSIGCONT\fR.

.TP
.BI limit " PID" " [" \-\-cpu " \fIPERCENT\fR] [" \-\-memory " \fIPERCENT\fR] [" \-\-remove "] [" \-\-yes "] [" \-\-dry\-run "]"
Apply or remove resource usage limits on a process using Linux cgroups v2.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " PERCENT"
Maximum CPU allocation percentage.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory " PERCENT"
Maximum memory quota as a percentage of system RAM.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
Remove existing cgroup resource restrictions for the process.
.RE

.TP
.BI renice " PID VALUE" " [" \-\-yes "] [" \-\-dry\-run "]"
Change the scheduling priority (nice value) of a process. Lower numbers indicate higher scheduling priority.

.TP
.BR report " [" \-\-last " \fIDURATION\fR] [" \-\-json "]"
Summarise system events, anomalies, and resource metrics over the designated historical window.

.TP
.BR daemon " [" \-\-duration " \fIDURATION\fR] [" \-\-background "] [" \-\-log\-file " \fIPATH\fR]"
Run
.B sentinel
as a background monitoring service.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " DURATION"
Total time to run before terminating.
.TP
.B \-\-background
Daemonize into the background.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " PATH"
File path to write daemon logs.
.RE

.TP
.BR update " [" \-\-check "] [" \-\-yes "]"
Check for or apply new releases from the repository.

.TP
.BR man " [" \-\-raw "]"
Show this manual page.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Output the raw troff/roff source without opening a pager or invoking a formatter.
.RE

.SH CONFIGURATION
.B sentinel
looks for its configuration file in the following order:
.IP 1.
The path specified in the \fBSENTINEL_CONFIG\fR environment variable.
.IP 2.
\fB/etc/sentinel/config.toml\fR
.IP 3.
\fB~/.config/sentinel/config.toml\fR

If no configuration file exists, sensible defaults are used.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sentinel/config.toml
System-wide configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sentinel/config.toml
User-level configuration file.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sentinel/sentinel.log
Default log file location when running as a systemd service.
.TP
.I /proc
Kernel process and system metric pseudo-filesystem.
.TP
.I /sys
Kernel device and subsystem pseudo-filesystem.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, an invalid argument was specified, or an operation was cancelled.

